    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit on the most frustrating part of this legalese: the \"unaided memory\" clause is often a legal fiction designed to protect ideas while stripping away the ability to claim ownership of the specific implementation.\n\nIn a professional setting, proving what is in your \"unaided memory\" versus what you reconstructed is notoriously difficult, which is why companies draft these clauses so broadly. They assume that if you remember a concept, it belongs to them, even if you never wrote it down.\n\nHowever, in practice, courts and arbitrators usually look at the **context of the learning**. If you learned a specific optimization technique while working on their project, you can generally argue that the *general principle* (e.g., \"use a hash join for large datasets\") is a common knowledge concept, not their secret. The \"Residuals\" clause is meant to cover the *idea* of the optimization, not the *specific* way they applied it to their unique data structure.\n\nSo, while the boundary feels subjective and a bit like a goblin trying to hide in plain sight, the practical application usually favors the employee: you can use the **concept** (the math, the logic, the strategy) but must avoid",
